# Release-age floor, in days. npm resolves a version range only to releases
# published more than this many days ago. A compromised publish is typically
# caught and unpublished within hours, well inside that window.
#
# npm applies the floor wherever it resolves a range, which includes npm
# install, npm update and every package-lock.json Renovate writes. When this
# file sets min-release-age, Renovate passes npm no --before of its own, so
# this value is the floor on those lockfiles. npm ci installs package-lock.json
# as written and does not re-check its entries. npm releases before 11.10.0 do
# not recognize the key and apply no floor.
#
# Keep this at or below minimumReleaseAge in the github>nanohype/.github
# Renovate preset. Renovate proposes a routine update once its release is that
# old, and a larger value here makes npm refuse the release Renovate proposes.
#
# Security fixes are held to this floor too. The preset's vulnerabilityAlerts
# block proposes a CVE fix at any age, but npm refuses a fix younger than this
# floor, so the fix waits until it is older than the floor. For a required
# dependency npm fails with ETARGET. For an optional dependency, such as
# @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk, npm instead leaves the package out of
# package-lock.json and exits 0, and npm ci rejects that lockfile.
# min-release-age-exclude (npm 11.17.0 and later) cannot narrow the floor to
# security fixes: it exempts packages by name or pattern, for every release.
#
# To take a release younger than the floor on purpose, such as that security
# fix, set its version in package.json and run:
#   npm install --min-release-age=0
# The command-line value overrides this file for that one run. npm keeps every
# locked entry that still satisfies package.json, so only the changed
# dependency and the packages it pulls in resolve without the floor.
min-release-age=3
